    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d644">

                <pb facs="0682" n="678" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg678"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.516/627: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-06-26T09:00:00+07:00">June 26, 1939—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 1:48 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>408. Department’s 113, June 23, 7 p.m. I took the occasion last evening to bring
                    this matter to attention of Kung who stated it was their desire to cooperate
                    with American banks and suggested that situation would be simplified if American
                    bankers joined Exchange <pb facs="0683" n="679" xml:id="pg679"/> Stabilization
                    Fund, from which they were buying exchange. He pointed out that French had
                    joined. It is my understanding that one of the purposes of recent visit of
                    French Ambassador to Chungking was to complete an arrangement whereby a French
                    contribution to the Stabilization Fund set up by British was made.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping, Shanghai.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
